<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Results on Large Models</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further evaluate our method on two large models, Qwen3-235B-A22B by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a></cite> and DeepSeek-V2.5-236B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI (2024)</a></cite>, see Tab. <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.1.1 Results on Large Models ‣ 3.1 Uncalibrated Uniform Quantization ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ SINQ: Sinkhorn-Normalized Quantization for Calibration-Free Low-Precision LLM Weights" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Notably, these are both MoE models, and the latter uses Multi-head Latent Attention (MLA). This underlines the robustness of SINQ to different architectures.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Weight-only PTQ on <span id="S3.T3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-V2.5-236B</span> and <span id="S3.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-235B-A22B</span> MoE models with 3-bit and 4-bit quantization, reporting perplexity and actual memory usage (GB). Lower is better for all metrics. The best result for a given setting is marked in <span id="S3.T3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>. </figcaption>
