</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Accuracy Results</h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Fig. <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.5 Accuracy Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SINQ: Sinkhorn-Normalized Quantization for Calibration-Free Low-Precision LLM Weights" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and Tab. <a href="#A1.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ A.5 Accuracy Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SINQ: Sinkhorn-Normalized Quantization for Calibration-Free Low-Precision LLM Weights" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> we report accuracy results on various QA tasks. Note that flips (as reported in the main paper) are the more reliable (and less easily manipulated) metric than accuracy for QA tasks, as shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dutta et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Fig. <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.5 Accuracy Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SINQ: Sinkhorn-Normalized Quantization for Calibration-Free Low-Precision LLM Weights" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> closely follows the analysis presented in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dutta et al. (2024)</a></cite>, further confirming the alignment of our findings with existing literature.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T10" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 10: </span>Accuracy (%) on HellaSwag, PIQA, and MMLU for Qwen3 models with 3-bit and 4-bit quantization. Higher is better.</figcaption>
