<section id="A1.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.7 </span>Additional Results on MoE Models</h3>

<div id="A1.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Tab. <a href="#A1.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ A.7 Additional Results on MoE Models ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SINQ: Sinkhorn-Normalized Quantization for Calibration-Free Low-Precision LLM Weights" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> we show some perplexity results on MoE models to underline the flexibility of our method. These results further demonstrate that SINQ is able to outperform state-of-the-art calibration-free methods for weight quantization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T12" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 12: </span>Weight-only PTQ on <span id="A1.T12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-V2-Lite</span> and <span id="A1.T12.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-30B-A3B</span> MoE models with 3-bit and 4-bit quantization, reporting perplexity and actual memory usage (GB). Lower is better for all metrics. In bold is the best result for a given setting.</figcaption>
